# Routh I.Arts.409-412 — the collinear three-body configuration, and its
# instability — dynamic notebook
#
# E. J. Routh, "A Treatise on Dynamics of a Particle" (Cambridge, 1898),
# Part I, Art. 409: "Three mutually attracting particles whose masses are M, m',
# m are placed in a straight line. It is required to determine the conditions
# that throughout their subsequent motion they may remain in a straight line."
#
# This is the second of Art. 408's two arrangements -- the collinear one, found
# by Euler, alongside Lagrange's equilateral. In general the spacing ratio is
# the root of an equation of degree 2k+1 (Art. 409), and Art. 410 notes the
# three cases -- the third mass between the other two, or beyond either -- must
# be treated separately, because "the analytical expression for the law of the
# inverse square does not represent the attraction" across a sign change.
#
# THE SYMMETRIC CASE, IN FULL. For three EQUAL masses the quintic collapses.
# By symmetry the middle body sits at the centre, the outer two at +/- d, and
# the middle one feels equal and opposite pulls -- it is in equilibrium and
# stays put. An outer body at +d feels
#
#     from the middle body (distance d)    :  G m^2 / d^2
#     from the far body    (distance 2d)   :  G m^2 / (4 d^2)
#
# both directed inward, so the total is (1 + 1/4) G m^2 / d^2. Matching that to
# the centripetal requirement m omega^2 d,
#
#     omega^2 = 1.25 * G m / d^3
#
# THE NUMBERS. G = 1, m = 1, d = 1:
#
#     omega  = sqrt(1.25) = 1.118033988749895
#     speed of each outer body = omega*d = 1.118033988749895
#     period = 2*pi/omega = 5.619851784832581
#
# The middle body has zero velocity and stays at the origin for ever.
#
# ART. 412 -- IT IS UNSTABLE. "Prove that if the force vary as any inverse
# power of the distance this state of motion is unstable." The second half of
# this notebook displaces one outer body by a thousandth and runs on: the
# spacing that was exact to 1e-13 stops being a spacing at all.
#
# SETUP NOTES. G = 1 and softening = 0 for the exact inverse square in the
# unperturbed run; the perturbed run uses the default softening, because the
# instability drives close approaches and an exact 1/r^2 singularity would end
# the integration rather than the demonstration.
#
# WHAT YOU WATCH: three bodies in a line, turning like a rigid rod about the
# middle one. Then the same line with one end nudged, which holds briefly and
# then tears itself apart.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LAUNCH (run from the repository root: /Users/youruser/Developer/code/rust/rustSimulate)
#
#     posim_notebook routh_p1_collinear_three_body
#
# or, without the shell alias, the always-available form:
#
#     cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p1_collinear_three_body.posim
#
# The scene window opens in your browser. To start the simulation, press Start
# in that window, or type this at the In[] prompt in the terminal:
#
#     scene start
#
# If no browser should be launched (the URL is printed instead), use:
#
#     POSIM_NO_BROWSER=1 cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p1_collinear_three_body.posim
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

set system.g_constant = 1
set system.softening = 0
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15

new point { mass = 1, position = [-1, 0, 0], velocity = [0, -1.118033988749895, 0] }
new point { mass = 1, position = [ 0, 0, 0] }
new point { mass = 1, position = [ 1, 0, 0], velocity = [0,  1.118033988749895, 0] }

# the middle body is in equilibrium; the outer two are symmetric
momentum
norm(obj0.position - obj1.position)
norm(obj2.position - obj1.position)

run 5.619851784832581 steps 8

# one full period: everything home, the middle body never having moved
norm(obj0.position - [-1, 0, 0])
norm(obj1.position - [0, 0, 0])
norm(obj2.position - [ 1, 0, 0])
reset

# ---- Art. 412: nudge one end by 0.001
set system.g_constant = 1
set system.softening = 1e-6
set system.rtol = 1e-12
set system.atol = 1e-14
new point { mass = 1, position = [-1.001, 0, 0], velocity = [0, -1.118033988749895, 0] }
new point { mass = 1, position = [ 0, 0, 0] }
new point { mass = 1, position = [ 1, 0, 0], velocity = [0,  1.118033988749895, 0] }

norm(obj0.position - obj1.position)
norm(obj2.position - obj1.position)
run 16.859555354497743 steps 12
# after three periods the two spacings no longer agree
norm(obj0.position - obj1.position)
norm(obj2.position - obj1.position)
run 33.719110708995486 steps 16
# after nine, the line is gone
norm(obj0.position - obj1.position)
norm(obj2.position - obj1.position)
reset

set system.g_constant = 1
set system.softening = 0
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15
new point { mass = 1, position = [-1, 0, 0], velocity = [0, -1.118033988749895, 0] }
new point { mass = 1, position = [ 0, 0, 0] }
new point { mass = 1, position = [ 1, 0, 0], velocity = [0,  1.118033988749895, 0] }
energy

scene create
scene zoom in
scene set_time_step 0.02
